EC2 Instance Purchasing Options —

When you launch an EC2 instance, AWS gives you different ways to pay for the compute capacity. The right option depends mainly on:

How long you need the server
Whether the workload is predictable
Whether interruption is acceptable
Whether you want flexibility

The major EC2 purchasing options are:

On-Demand Instances
Reserved Instances (RI)
Savings Plans
Spot Instances
Dedicated Hosts
Dedicated Instances
Capacity Reservations

1. On-Demand Instances:
Simple meaning

You pay for EC2 capacity as you use it, with no long-term commitment.

Think:

"I need a server now. I'll pay for the time I use it."

Real-time example

Suppose your company has a production application:

Users
  ↓
ALB
  ↓
EC2
  ↓
RDS

You don't know exactly how long you'll need a particular EC2 instance.

You launch:

t3.medium

and run it for 100 hours.

You pay for those 100 hours according to the applicable On-Demand rate.

When to use

Good for:

Development
Testing
Short-term projects
Unpredictable workloads
Applications where you don't want a commitment
DevOps example

You are testing a new Jenkins pipeline.

You create:

EC2 → t3.medium
Jenkins

After testing, you terminate the instance.

On-Demand makes sense because you don't need the server continuously.

Interview point

On-Demand = flexibility, no commitment, higher price compared with commitment-based options.


2. Reserved Instances (RI):
Reserved Instances are useful when you know you'll need EC2 capacity for a long period.

Typically, you commit to a specific configuration/region/term depending on the RI type, in exchange for a lower effective price.

Common commitment periods:

1 year
3 years
Real-time example

Suppose your company has:

Production Application
        ↓
EC2

and you know:

"This application will run 24×7 for the next 3 years."

Instead of paying the full On-Demand rate continuously, you can use a Reserved Instance commitment.

Simple analogy

Imagine renting a room:

On-Demand

Pay every day.

Reserved

Commit to a longer period and receive a lower effective rate.

Important

Reserved Instances are not actually a different EC2 machine type.

They are primarily a billing/discount mechanism tied to eligible EC2 usage.

When to use

Use RI when:

Workload = predictable
Duration = long

Example:

Production database server
Production application server
Internal application running 24×7


3. Savings Plans:
Savings Plans are another way to get discounted compute pricing through a commitment to a consistent amount of compute spend per hour over a term.

This is extremely important for modern AWS environments.

There are two major types:

Compute Savings Plans
EC2 Instance Savings Plans
Compute Savings Plans

More flexible.

Your commitment can apply across:

EC2
Lambda
Fargate

and provides flexibility across eligible compute usage.

Real-time example

Today:

EC2
m5.large

Six months later you migrate to:

EC2
m7i.large

A Compute Savings Plan can provide more flexibility than a traditional RI commitment.

Think:

"I know approximately how much compute I'll continuously use, but I don't want to lock myself too tightly to one configuration."

EC2 Instance Savings Plans

These provide a discount in exchange for commitment to a consistent amount of usage for a particular EC2 instance family in a region.

Example:

Region: ap-south-1

Instance family:
M family

Commitment:
₹X/hour

You may change eligible instance sizes within the family while retaining the benefit, subject to the plan rules.


4. Spot Instances ⭐

This is very important for AWS DevOps interviews.

Spot Instances allow you to use AWS's spare EC2 capacity at potentially large discounts compared with On-Demand pricing.

But:

AWS can interrupt Spot capacity when it needs the capacity back.

Therefore:

Cheap
  ↓
Spot
  ↓
Can be interrupted
Real-time DevOps example

Imagine you're processing:

10,000 images

Each image can be processed independently.

You don't care if one EC2 instance disappears because the job can restart elsewhere.

Architecture:

S3
 ↓
SQS
 ↓
EC2 Auto Scaling Group
 ↓
Image Processing

You can use Spot Instances.

If AWS interrupts one instance:

Spot Instance
      X
   interrupted
      ↓
ASG launches replacement
      ↓
Job continues
Good workloads

Spot is excellent for workloads that are:

Fault tolerant
Distributed
Restartable
Flexible about timing

Examples:

Batch processing
Big-data processing
CI/CD build workers
Container workloads
Image/video processing
Data analysis
Testing environments



5. Spot + ASG = Powerful DevOps Pattern

This is a very common real-world architecture.

Suppose you have Jenkins.

Instead of permanently running:

10 × EC2 On-Demand

you could have:

Jenkins
   ↓
Auto Scaling Group
   ↓
Spot EC2
   ↓
Build jobs

If demand increases:

5 workers
   ↓
10 workers

ASG can scale out.

When demand decreases:

10 workers
   ↓
2 workers

ASG scales in.

This can significantly reduce compute cost when your workload tolerates interruptions.



6. Dedicated Hosts

A Dedicated Host is a physical EC2 server dedicated to your AWS account.

You get visibility/control over the physical host and can place eligible instances on it.

Real-time example

Suppose a company has software with a licensing model based on:

Physical CPU/socket/core usage.

The company may need to know exactly which physical server its instances are running on.

Dedicated Hosts can help with such licensing requirements.

Think:
Normal EC2

AWS physical hardware
 ├── Customer A
 ├── Customer B
 └── Customer C


Dedicated Host

AWS physical hardware
 └── YOUR AWS ACCOUNT
      ├── EC2
      ├── EC2
      └── EC2
Use cases
Certain software licensing requirements
Compliance requirements
Host-level visibility/control



7. Dedicated Instances:
Dedicated Instances run on hardware dedicated to a single AWS customer, but unlike Dedicated Hosts, you don't get the same level of visibility/control over the physical host.

Think:
Dedicated Instance
        ↓
Hardware dedicated to your account

But:

Dedicated Host
        ↓
Hardware dedicated to your account
        +
Host-level management/visibility


8. Capacity Reservations

This one is frequently confused with Reserved Instances.

Capacity Reservation

Capacity Reservation means:

Reserve EC2 capacity in a specific Availability Zone so that the capacity is available when you need it.

It is primarily about capacity availability, not necessarily getting a discount.

Example

Suppose your production application requires:

10 × m6i.large

in:

ap-south-1a

You are concerned that during a critical event you might not be able to obtain the required capacity.

You can create a Capacity Reservation.

Conceptually:

AZ: ap-south-1a

Reserved capacity
 ├── EC2
 ├── EC2
 ├── EC2
 └── ...
Key distinction

Reserved Instance

"Give me a pricing benefit for committed usage."

Capacity Reservation

"Make sure EC2 capacity is available in this AZ."



| Option                   | Commitment               | Can be interrupted? | Main purpose                   |
| ------------------------ | ------------------------ | ------------------: | ------------------------------ |
| **On-Demand**            | No                       |                  No | Flexible usage                 |
| **Reserved Instance**    | 1/3-year commitment      |                  No | Discount for predictable usage |
| **Savings Plan**         | Spend commitment         |                  No | Discount + flexibility         |
| **Spot**                 | No                       |             **Yes** | Very low-cost flexible compute |
| **Dedicated Host**       | Depends on billing model |                 No* | Dedicated physical host        |
| **Dedicated Instance**   | No                       |                 No* | Hardware dedicated to account  |
| **Capacity Reservation** | Reservation commitment   |                 No* | Ensure EC2 capacity in an AZ   |



